A||||6||mshill||Shift cable heat shielding? Fuel Lines?||||5||
Z||000000||mshill||01-25-2001||04:58 PM||||I am trying to clean up my engine compartment before I shove my 3.4L into it and was wondering what people have done to for the shift cable heat shields?  The foil on mine is flaking off all over the place and they look ratty.  What can be done?<P>Also, I was wondering if anyone has replaced the two fuel lines on the V6 with flexible stainless?  Where can I get high pressure stainless braided fuel lines.<P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Mark||01-25-2001||06:16 PM||||Well, I asked this same question about 2 months ago and got no response. Maybe I should have asked about a V12 swap or a Boeing hood scoop or something  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Anyway, I had the same problem with flaking shifter cable insulation.  My solution was to wrap the shifter cable insulation with adhesive-backed aluminum tape.  Looks great!<BR>The stuff I used was actually marketed as a "tempory fix" for mufflers or auto body rust repair.  The tape is about 2" wide, quite thin and flexible, about the thickness of heavy aluminum foil.  I applied it in a spiral with about a 50% overlap.  I also applied end pieces square to the ends of the insulation.  I can't say how it will hold up under the hood, but I figured the glue should hold up well under heat since they claim it can be used for muffler repairs.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||01-25-2001||07:14 PM||||You can get the shield tape and the line material at most speed shops, jegs, etc.... <P>You'll probably need to convert to AN fittings to use the braded line on Fiero. There are various adaptors for this but I don't specifically know what ones you'll need.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Wannabe||01-25-2001||08:22 PM||||About the hose, a well equipped industrial supply house can usually sell you whatever you need. I saved over $100 on a small section of fuel hose for one of my cars. The rubber hose from factory hose is known to fail, so I had a supply hose use the same fitting and high pressure ss braid hose to make up a new one. $15 later I had a part that wil easily outlast the $120 oe part.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||01-26-2001||09:08 AM||||The fuel lines are rigid to keep them out of the A/C compressor pulley.  If you go with flexible be sure they are properly restrained to keep them off the pulley.<P>Post some pics when you are through if you can.<P>Take care,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000005||Alex4mula||01-26-2001||03:38 PM||||On my 3.4 conversion I did exactly what Mark did. Then I found out a local speed shop that sells heat shield sleeves just like the ones in the shift cable. Also I saw them either in Jegs or Summit catalogs or on both. They look a lot better and more stock. Not that expensive also.||||reg||1
Z||000006||mshill||01-28-2001||03:00 PM||||Thanks for the info guys.  I found the heat shield sleeving for the shift cables at my local speed shop.  It was $20 for 36 inches.  Cut in half they fit perfect on the two shift cables.<P>As for the rigid fuel lines I didn't find anything but I was going to paint them and while cleaning them up found that a little 400 grit sandpaper cleaned them up real good. <P>It all is starting to look pretty good.||||reg||1
